# WHAT DOES NOT GET UPLOADED TO CLOUD BUILD.
#
# `gcloud builds submit` tars this directory and sends it to Google. Without a
# .gcloudignore it falls back to .gitignore semantics, which would happen to
# cover most of this — but "happens to" is not how a file containing live
# credentials and real customer media should be excluded. Every line below is
# stated on purpose, and the first two are the ones that matter.

# LIVE SECRETS. REDIS_URL, DATABASE_URL with its password, the Blob token, the
# field encryption key, API keys. Nothing in the build needs any of them: the
# gateway takes its configuration from Cloud Run env vars at boot and refuses to
# start if a password reaches it at all.
.env
.env.*

# REAL CUSTOMER FILES, exported from production by the media migration. They are
# on disk only until the move is proven and must not leave this machine.
media-export/

# The dev-only login route. Git-ignored so it can never deploy; the same is true
# here, and for the same reason.
src/app/api/dev-login/

# Build output and dependencies — the image installs its own from the lockfile,
# so uploading these would only make the tarball enormous and the build slower.
node_modules/
services/pg-gateway/node_modules/
services/pg-gateway/dist/
.next/
out/

# History, tooling and scratch. None of it reaches the image.
.git/
.github/
.claude/
.superpowers/
coverage/
*.log

# The app's own surface. The gateway's image needs services/pg-gateway and
# src/platform/db, and nothing else in here — but the build context has to be
# the repo root for those two to be visible at once, so the rest rides along
# unless it is named. These are the large ones.
public/
docs/
legal/
tests/goldens/
